%N A397656 a(n) is the smallest k such that A397224(k) = n or -1 if no such k exists.
%C A397656 I conjecture that k will always exist.
%C A397656 a(24) = 175456512.
%C A397656 From _David A. Corneth_, Jul 03 2026, Jul 05 2026, Jul 07 2026: (Start)
%C A397656 a(n) is a perfect square if and only if n is odd.
%C A397656 a(23) <= 5404225860633600, a(25) <= 3058244841960000, a(27) <= 247717832198760000, a(29) <= 63415765042882560000, a(32) = 4055646672.
%C A397656 a(31) <= 2601715844446709760000, a(33) <= 41627453511147356160000, a(35) <= 1626072402779193600000000, a(37) <= 26017158444467097600000000.
%C A397656 For odd n we can write a(n) = s^2 = m*(m + k^2) = m^2 + m*k^2. Completing the square we get s^2 + k^4/4 = ((m + k^2)/2)^2 so k^4/4 = ((m + k^2)/2)^2 - s^2 = (m + k^2/2 - s)*(m + k^2/2 + s). If k is odd then we multiply by 4.
%C A397656 When k is even k^4/4 is even and we may list the divisors of k^4/4 = x*y and have x = m + k^2/2 - s and y = m + k^2/2 + s where x and y are divisors of k^4/4. As x and y must have same parity and x*y is even we can choose x,y of divisors of k^4/16 and multiply the divisors by 2.
%C A397656 Using the method outlined above the listed a(n) for odd n are above if at least one of the values k is <= 222000 and even. (End)
%C A397656 a(28) = 801115392, a(30) <= 20967609600. - _Michael S. Branicky_, Jul 04 2026
%C A397656 a(34) = 12817846272. - _Chai Wah Wu_, Aug 05 2026
